#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "515ab620-8513-55cc-8c88-61c0ad27aff2" :question/math-academy-id "e-2833" :question/problem "Which of the following terms describe the shape below?\n\nI. Parallelogram\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Square\n\n![](images/d5/d578ca7af1f030dabb8e44a5a8fab90ce4bd21d002054fccdf2be2413a855a5c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent angles, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- Since all the angles in this parallelogram are right, it is a rectangle.\n\n- Not all the sides of this rectangle are congruent, so it is not a square.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram) and II (rectangle)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3a028c-93df-5e5a-bc06-4c286a5e1839" :question/math-academy-id "e-2834" :question/problem "Which of the following shapes is a trapezoid?\n\n![](images/6a/6a9f79e60fb5fca0ffee8fed594c19e946d3d6f02544f65ebd7e040e5cbf1ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a trapezoid is a quadrilateral with exactly one pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid since it has one pair of opposite sides that are parallel.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rhombus. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{R}$ is a kite. Its opposite sides are not parallel, therefore it is not a trapezoid.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{S}$ is a rectangle. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\nTherefore, only shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"}] :question/id #uuid "a7b82dde-36fc-582e-9ed6-fbfcfbf8b47c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2839" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A rhombus is always a kite.\n\nII. A rhombus is always a parallelogram.\n\nIII. A kite is always a parallelogram." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A rhombus fits this description, because all the sides in a rhombus are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. The same is true for a rhombus. A rhombus is a specific type of parallelogram, where all the sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement III is false.  In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. However, in a kite, it's only necessary that two pairs of congruent sides are adjacent to each other. Therefore, a kite is not always a parallelogram.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274c9f91-07ea-5d83-8c64-64d15923d767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446820} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc4133d-8cbc-54e2-9bb1-8bef4ee6f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446822} {:answer/id #uuid "9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446833} {:answer/id #uuid "d7345b8a-0546-52d8-b568-a7582dd09262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446837} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fbe29d-ac6e-50df-9af8-75419d3d96e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446833} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5d2df3-6e2f-5d97-b571-87478c1a4929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef405c5c-f229-5ca4-acda-70f35ff263e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-128157" :question/problem "![](images/da/da2336d6c4360d10fedbfc6441280b5a08091df34f3f59e2e94e7d4daf01bc3f.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Trapezoid\n\nIII. Rhombus" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where all sides are congruent and all angles are right, so it is a square.\n\n- Since the quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair, it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. So it is a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (square)  and III (rhombus)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50412497-1363-5a7a-bc67-c737bddc5db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446825} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446827} {:answer/id #uuid "83868f10-611f-563b-8743-8cf6e8a7dedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446829} {:answer/id #uuid "9d834235-9a54-5464-b64a-7b2cad018124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446834} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c89198-c72f-537e-aef1-1c2295f53343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446827} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1134a5b-29fb-5dbe-a5b8-c1fc4bb904c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6a1177b-8e6f-5855-8414-02d987aa4107" :question/math-academy-id "q-128251" :question/problem "![](images/78/781e1e9f5a08cf7f01d9896e957f8d983cd428817e3a5eeb90312a89fdc0a932.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a  rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. Since the sides are not all congruent, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus since all its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of congruent sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a rhombus."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460e7f6c-aa47-56f6-b331-546223765a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $S$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446823} {:answer/id #uuid "472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446824} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5d7662-6751-5ba8-a72b-f701f02b9659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446831} {:answer/id #uuid "ba482929-e458-5616-88b5-17d64aa90840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446836} {:answer/id #uuid "d95ed278-9540-5cb0-91d2-fa17ecfaa247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446824} :answer-field/id #uuid "25a7aabb-2b7a-53d0-b50c-ce5b5db78d9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d149b00e-3076-56e3-b5b0-ca0e159fc928" :question/math-academy-id "q-128291" :question/problem "![](images/94/94a6607f6d12c3c59c9822d91dfb6c6b8b3a8d23bba9ef1c3a7253be564f95d8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. There is a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since every pair of opposite sides in a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite in which no pair of opposite sides are parallel, so it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not parallelograms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaeafc54-7b92-5062-9bdb-15d818ddc76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109286} {:answer/id #uuid "e459090c-5c9d-5d00-95e2-d581d6447dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109287} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d9a4f1-68e6-5d47-8c82-5c0b4c9aa084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109288} {:answer/id #uuid "35964d8d-753e-5e39-9f1d-9b0c320a166c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109289} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd5188-95f9-5c2b-b965-cff6cf0912dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e459090c-5c9d-5d00-95e2-d581d6447dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109287} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5fa196-47ec-5e14-99ea-eb0655130b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "599179be-ddc2-50ab-a431-64168ebbcf12" :question/math-academy-id "q-53000" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bffbc53c9e35da03655638ca40c5899bc5290cbadf3d14aef8e82585f14e004.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Kite\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Trapezoid" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair. So it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (kite), and II (rectangle)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc9f265f-b2fd-5a80-9c41-ff41fc5c7c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109298} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bcf564-cd85-5256-8741-95133c0620a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109299} {:answer/id #uuid "f1400078-ec15-50b3-aae7-004a1df11356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109300} {:answer/id #uuid "010cdc97-1ea2-55c2-ab55-d192ea2266a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109301} {:answer/id #uuid "0564d3dd-6b67-5c98-b35a-5246353f9baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc9f265f-b2fd-5a80-9c41-ff41fc5c7c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109298} :answer-field/id #uuid "e187a24b-d5d5-548e-a10f-715cef7faf9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c9c084b-0547-549b-b2ae-ba90597387ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-53010" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b68f8d3d0634999697051b668c06dd34c1191905a7bebda79925cd437d454d36.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the trapezoid above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\nII. $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{AD}∥\\overset{―}{BC}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The trapezoid has a pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We can see that $∠ABC$ is a right angle, that is, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The trapezoid has one pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446826} {:answer/id #uuid "74c6cb69-f0a7-5ef4-9ddd-04b2362f40f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446828} {:answer/id #uuid "85e91a34-837c-56d1-8278-c12f595fa8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446830} {:answer/id #uuid "b9056c57-907f-5eec-9a56-d31436b0f16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446835} {:answer/id #uuid "e87d5322-2b09-569e-af79-49e11b59bcb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446826} :answer-field/id #uuid "a86d07c0-4185-50bb-bf78-3ee1ddde8cc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fe04944-0102-5a76-9a34-b22553e0ce91" :question/math-academy-id "q-53060" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is always a kite.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a square.\n\nIII. A rhombus is always a trapezoid." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A square fits the description because all the sides in a square are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is false. In a square, all the sides are congruent. However, in a rectangle, it's only necessary that two pairs of opposite sides are congruent to each other. Therefore, a rectangle is not always a square.\n\n- Statement III is false. In a trapezoid, only $1$ pair of opposite sides are parallel to each other. However, in a rhombus, the $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel to each other. Therefore, a rhombus is not a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dad930-3bec-5023-9f09-d5e37c38df03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446819} {:answer/id #uuid "2793e408-578a-5a06-b01c-25af3c2f51ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446821} {:answer/id #uuid "937c2af6-40ad-55ca-b87d-3c33e8fb38c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446832} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c9ddeb-40d8-5c69-bdf1-cf098a254efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446841} {:answer/id #uuid "f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446843} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd86c5b-5668-52b2-9022-45e2df077d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "334188d1-1448-5a61-bc9c-b781073c37c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74510" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3e39478d45abcbd97722616961adcd3a10aaa9da78845b22ea9566b324fa34.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Rhombus\n\nIII. Parallelogram" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with angles that are not all right. So it is **not** a square.\n\n- Since the four sides of the quadrilateral are congruent, it is a rhombus.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a rhombus. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all four sides are equal. So it is a parallelogram.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms II (rhombus), and III (parallelogram)."}]}
